// @test: run
// @tier: 0
// @level: L1
// @tags: fixed-size-array, annotation, silent-wrong-value
// @expect: pass
// @expected-stdout: 1
// @description: A fixed-size array annotation on a `let` must not change the values bound.

// =============================================================================
// Measured 2026-09-03 — separating pair, one difference
// =============================================================================
//
//     let [x,y,z]: [Int;3] = three();   ->  0 0 0
//     let [x,y,z]           = three();   ->  1 2 3
//
// The same file, one `sed` substitution apart. Four neighbours are all
// correct, so it needs the annotation AND a call initialiser together:
//
//     literal WITH the annotation      1 2 3
//     literal without                  1 2 3
//     fn call without the annotation   1 2 3
//     indexing a[0], a[1], a[2]        1 2 3
//
// NARROWED FURTHER after the first write-up: destructuring is NOT
// involved. The same zeroing happens with a plain binding and indexing,
// and it needs THREE things together — a fixed-size array annotation, a
// `let`, and a CALL initialiser:
//
//     [Int;3] annotation + fn call      0 0    <- the defect
//     [Int;3] annotation + literal      1 3
//     no annotation      + fn call      1 3
//     List<Int> annotation + fn call    1 3
//     Int annotation     + fn call      7 7
//
// Tuple and record destructures with annotations and call initialisers
// are correct too. This file keeps the destructuring form because that
// is how it was found, and asserts the plain form beside it.
//
// A type annotation is a claim about a type. Here it silently replaces
// the VALUES.

fn three() -> [Int; 3] {
    [1, 2, 3]
}

fn main() {
    let [x, y, z]: [Int; 3] = three();
    print(x);

    // The same defect without any pattern at all.
    let a: [Int; 3] = three();
    print(a[0]);
}
